notes.body:
Concurrency means making progress on multiple things at once, and Python offers three models. Threads share one memory space — cheap to start, but shared state needs locks and a crash in one thread can take the process. Processes (multiprocessing) each get their own memory and their own GIL — true parallelism for CPU-bound work, at the cost of heavier startup and inter-process communication. Async (asyncio) runs many tasks on a single thread, switching at await points — extremely efficient for I/O-bound work (thousands of connections), but one blocking call with no await freezes everything. Worked example — I/O-bound fetching with threads:

import threading, time

def fetch(n):
    time.sleep(1)  # stands in for network I/O
    print(f"done {n}")

threads = [threading.Thread(target=fetch, args=(i,)) for i in range(4)]
for t in threads: t.start()
for t in threads: t.join()
print("all done")

Solution: prints "done 0" through "done 3" in some order after about one second total, then "all done" — the four sleeps overlap because threads release the GIL during I/O. The same work sequentially would take four seconds. Rule of thumb: threads or async for I/O-bound (API calls, DB queries), processes for CPU-bound (number crunching, image work), async when you need massive connection counts with low overhead. Picking wrong is a panel-visible defect: threads for CPU-bound hashing gain nothing under the GIL.

Kubernetes has three probes with three different consequences, and mixing them up causes real outages. Liveness: "is this container dead?" If the liveness probe fails, the kubelet RESTARTS the container. Readiness: "should this pod receive traffic?" If it fails, the pod is removed from service endpoints but NOT restarted. Startup: for slow-starting containers, it gates the other two so a long warmup does not trigger restarts. Worked example — a Python API with correct probe design:

@app.get("/healthz")   # liveness: trivially cheap
def healthz():
    return {"status": "ok"}

@app.get("/ready")     # readiness: checks real dependencies
def ready():
    if not db.ping(timeout=2):
        raise HTTPException(503, "db unreachable")
    return {"status": "ready"}

The liveness endpoint does no I/O at all — it answers even when the database is down, because a dead database is not a dead container and restarting would not help. The readiness endpoint checks the database with a short timeout, so traffic stops flowing to a pod that cannot serve. The classic defect is one /health endpoint doing deep dependency checks wired as the liveness probe: a database blip then restarts every pod instead of just pausing traffic. Panel rule: liveness = cheap and restart-worthy; readiness = dependency-aware and traffic-gating; startup = warmup grace period.

Every network call your service makes needs a timeout, or one slow dependency becomes your outage. The failure mode: your API calls a downstream service with no timeout; the downstream hangs; your request handlers pile up waiting; memory and threads exhaust; readiness probes time out; the kubelet pulls the pod. One hung dependency, full cascade. Worked example — the timeout discipline in a readiness check:

def db_reachable():
    try:
        return db.ping(timeout=2)   # 2s, not 30s, not forever
    except TimeoutError:
        return False

Three timeout layers professionals set. One: client timeouts on every outbound call (requests timeout=, DB connect_timeout) — short for probes, generous for batch jobs. Two: server timeouts — how long your framework waits before abandoning a request handler, so one bad request cannot hold a worker forever. Three: probe timeouts in the pod spec (timeoutSeconds) shorter than the period, so a stuck probe fails fast instead of overlapping. Also set connection-pool limits: unlimited outbound connections under load become a self-inflicted DDoS on your own database. The panel-ready summary: "timeouts convert hangs into errors, and errors are retryable, alertable, and survivable — hangs are none of those." When reviewing any service, ask "where is the timeout?" on every network call; a missing one is a finding.

Go's workhorse data structures are slices (dynamic arrays) and maps (hash tables), with deliberately small control-flow syntax: for is the only loop, if requires no parentheses, switch is clean. Worked example:

package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    hosts := []string{"web1", "web2", "web3"}
    status := map[string]string{"web1": "up", "web2": "down"}
    fmt.Println(len(hosts))
    fmt.Println(status["web2"])
    for i, h := range hosts {
        fmt.Printf("%d: %s\n", i, h)
    }
}

Solution: prints 3, then "down", then "0: web1", "1: web2", "2: web3". Walkthrough: []string{"web1", ...} is a slice literal; map[string]string maps strings to strings; len works on slices; range yields index and value. Three sharp edges: indexing a missing map key returns the zero value ("" here) with no error — use the two-value form v, ok := status["web9"] to detect absence; slices share backing arrays, so appending may or may not reallocate (copy when sharing matters); and range over a map visits keys in random order — never depend on map iteration order in output. For conditionals, if err != nil is the heartbeat of Go code (next note), and switch needs no break statements.

Go has no exceptions. Functions return errors as ordinary values — usually the last return value — and callers check them explicitly. This makes failure handling visible in the code instead of hidden in stack unwinding, which is exactly what you want in automation that must degrade gracefully. Worked example:

package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "strconv"
)

func parsePort(s string) (int, error) {
    return strconv.Atoi(s)
}

func main() {
    port, err := parsePort("8080")
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println("bad port:", err)
        return
    }
    fmt.Println("port:", port)
}

Solution: prints "port: 8080". Walkthrough: parsePort returns (int, error); the caller binds both and branches on err != nil before touching port. The idiom if err != nil { ... } appears after nearly every fallible call — reading Go means scanning those branches to learn every failure mode the author considered. Conventions: error is the last return value; functions that cannot fail do not return one; sentinel errors and fmt.Errorf("dial %s: %w", addr, err) with %w wrap context around root causes while preserving unwrapping. For CLIs, a fatal error usually means print to stderr and os.Exit(1) — nonzero exit codes are how automation detects your tool's failure, the same contract as the Python CLI in M19.
